Given a number $n$, we now need to find the number of permutations of $n, n+1$, ..., $n-1$, subject to the restriction that the next number in the permutation must be equal to or above that current number after subtracting $1$.
 We now replace $n$ by $k$ and get $k, k+1, ..., k+n$. We now want to find the number of permutations of this set from version 2 or the modified one that fit against one another.
The answer would only be in terms of $n$
 after times the number of permutations that fit the original requirement of version 1 (since we are ignoring the change we made in version 2).
 We get that the number must in fact depend on the following sum:
\begin{align*}
 \sum^{2n}_{k=1}k^2 (2n)! &= (2n)(2n+1)(2n-1)(2n-1)(2n-1)(2n-1)(2n-1)(2n-1)(2n-1) \\
     &=(2n)(2n+1)(4n^3-7n^2+5n-1)
\end{align*}
 We must now find $(2n)(2n+1)(4n^3-7n^2+5n-1) \bmod{1000} \forall n \in \Bbb Z, 1 \le n \le 6 \land n \in \Bbb Z$.
 for n = 1
 \begin{align*}
          2(2+1)(4-7+5-1) &= 2 \bmod{1000}
      \end{align*}
for n =2
 \begin{align*}
          2(2+1)(4(2)^3-7(2)^2+5(2)-1)&= 240 \bmod{1000}
       \end{align*}
for n = 3
 \begin{align*}
          2(2+1)(4(3)^3-7(3)^2+5(3)-1)&= 3780 \bmod{1000}
       \end{align*}
now multiply each by 8, as the base start cube cubes are now worth 2 instead of 1, then take the remainder, then add the two results
 \begin{align*}
           2(2+1)(4(1)^3-7(1)^2+5(1)-1)(8^1)+2(2+1)(4(2)^3-7(2)^2+5(2)-1)(8^2)+2(2+1)(4(3)^3-7(3)^2+5(3)-1)(8^3)&= 8+480+3780 &\bmod{1000}
       \end{align*}
 our required number is $8+480+3780 = \color{red}{4368} \bmod{1000} = \color{red}{368}$
